<BaseLayout title="Death Valley, Vegas &amp; Page — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Five days in March 2022: Death Valley, Las Vegas, Horseshoe Bend at sunrise, Lake Powell and the light beams of Upper Antelope Canyon, with a phone that could not decide what time it was.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · March 2022</p>
  <h1>Death Valley, Vegas &amp; Page</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Horseshoe Bend at sunrise, Antelope Canyon at noon, and three clocks that never agreed.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>5 days · March 12–16, 2022</strong><br />
    Los Angeles ✈ Las Vegas → Pahrump → Death Valley → Las Vegas → Page, Arizona → Las Vegas ✈ Los Angeles<br />
    Roughly 1,100 miles by rental car · 4 states, plus the Navajo Nation · from 282 feet below sea level to the top
    of an 1,149-foot tower · one switch to daylight saving time, and two neighbors that disagree about it
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-overview.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-overview.webp'} alt="Map of the whole trip: a flight from Los Angeles to Las Vegas, a loop through Death Valley from Pahrump, a drive east to Page, Arizona, and back to Las Vegas." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole loop. The blue dashed line is the flight; red is the rental car.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    This was a short trip with a lot of geology in it. In five days in March 2022 we flew from Los Angeles to Las
    Vegas, picked up a rental car, and drove it to two of the most photographed landscapes in the American
    Southwest and back. I didn't have a driver's license yet, so a friend did all of the driving, and I did the
    looking out of the window. The first was Death Valley, the lowest, driest and hottest national park in the country,
    which in March is merely warm. The second was Page, Arizona, a small town on the edge of Lake Powell, which sits
    within a short drive of Horseshoe Bend and Antelope Canyon. In between, and at both ends, was Las Vegas,
    which turns out to be a very convenient place to start a desert road trip and a very strange place to finish one.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other trip posts here, everything below is rebuilt from the camera roll: where and when each photo
    was taken, lined up in order. That record has gaps, and where it is silent I have kept quiet too. It also
    holds one small puzzle, which turned out to be the most interesting thing in the metadata. Every phone photo is
    stamped with its offset from universal time, and on this trip those offsets jump around in a way that no single
    clock on the wall would. Daylight saving time began in the middle of the trip. And the trip ended up in the
    one corner of the country where a ten-minute drive can move you an hour forward or back. On the road to Page the
    phone's clock changed several times, and at the time it was genuinely confusing. More on that in Page.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Death Valley at sunset</h2>
  <p>
    We landed in Las Vegas on the morning of Saturday, March 12th, and drove west over the Spring Mountains to
    <strong>Pahrump</strong>, a sprawling Nevada town of desert lots and wide roads that serves, among other things,
    as the closest real town to the eastern side of Death Valley. It was our base for the first night, at the Best Western Pahrump Oasis. Lunch, at 2:21 in
    the afternoon, was a bowl of curry and rice, and then we headed for the park.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0312.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0312.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 1: from the Las Vegas airport to Pahrump, then Zabriskie Point at sunset and Dante's View after dark." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: the airport, Pahrump, then Zabriskie Point at sunset and Dante's View after dark.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Zabriskie Point</h3>
  <p>
    The road from Pahrump crosses into California and drops toward the valley, and the first stop on the way down,
    a little after five, was <strong>Zabriskie Point</strong>. A short paved walk leads up from the parking lot to a
    stone-walled overlook, and below it the ground falls away into badlands: a sea of bare, folded hills in pale gold
    and cream, without a plant on them, furrowed by gullies so regular they look combed. In the low evening sun every
    ridge had a bright side and a shadow side, and the whole landscape looked like crumpled paper.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zabriskie-badlands.webp'} alt="Pale gold badland hills furrowed by gullies under a clear blue sky, with dark mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Zabriskie Point badlands, 5:18 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zabriskie-ridges.webp'} alt="Rows of furrowed badland ridges below the stone wall of the Zabriskie Point overlook." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The overlook's stone wall, and the ridges beyond it.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Those hills are old lake mud. A few million years ago this part of the valley held a lake, and the clay, silt
    and volcanic ash that settled on its bottom became the soft rock of what geologists call the Furnace Creek
    Formation. Then the land was tilted and lifted, the lake vanished, and the rare desert rain went to work on the
    rock. Because nothing grows here to hold it in place, every storm cuts the gullies a little deeper. The point
    itself is named after Christian Zabriskie, who ran the Pacific Coast Borax Company's operations here in the early
    twentieth century, when the valley's main business was digging up borax and hauling it out by mule team. It also
    lent its name to a 1970 Antonioni film, which is how many Europeans first heard of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turn around and the view is different: across the mouth of the badlands to the valley floor and the bare brown
    mountains on the far side, with the parking lot a little cluster of cars far below. The light at this time of
    day makes the desert look soft, though nothing here is soft at all. By half past five the overlook wall was lined
    with people facing west, phones up, waiting.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zabriskie-valley.webp'} alt="View from Zabriskie Point down to a parking lot and across a desert valley to brown mountains in evening light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking back toward the road and the mountains.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The sun went down behind the <strong>Panamint Range</strong>, the long wall of mountains on the west side of
    Death Valley, at about 5:31 by our clocks. The badlands went from gold to gray in a few minutes. For a moment the
    sun sat in a notch of the ridge line with a lens flare blooming beside it, and then there was just a band of
    yellow along the mountains and the cold blue of desert dusk above. In the desert, the temperature drops as fast as
    the light does.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zabriskie-sunset.webp'} alt="The sun setting over the Panamint Range above the darkening badlands, with a lens flare." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset over the Panamints, 5:31 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zabriskie-last-light.webp'} alt="The last sliver of sun on the mountain ridge, with the badlands in shadow below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The last sliver, three minutes later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Dante's View, after dark</h3>
  <p>
    Then we drove up to <strong>Dante's View</strong>, at the end of a long, winding road that climbs the Black
    Mountains on the east side of the valley. The overlook stands at about 5,500 feet, and on a clear day you look
    almost straight down a mile of air to Badwater, the lowest point in North America, with the snow on Telescope
    Peak across the valley at eye level. We got there at 7:31, well after dark, so none of that was visible. What
    was visible was the sky.
  </p>
  <p>
    Death Valley is an International Dark Sky Park, one of the darkest places in the lower 48, and on a moonless
    night the Milky Way is bright enough to throw a faint glow on the ground. This was not a moonless night. The moon
    was a few days past first quarter, and its glare washed out a corner of every frame. The phone did its best
    through the car window and then outside it, and the results are a scatter of small white dots on a navy
    background. They are not good pictures. But they record the hour and the cold, and in the dark at the edge of
    a drop you can't see, a scatter of white dots is more or less what it looks like anyway.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'dantes-stars.webp'} alt="A dark blue night sky with a scattering of stars, faintly washed by moonlight." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The phone's best effort at Dante's View, 7:33 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The drive back to Pahrump took most of the next two hours. Dinner, at 9:18, was a cup of instant noodles.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 2: the bottom of the valley</h2>
  <p>
    Sunday, March 13th, began with a small mystery in the metadata. Saturday's photos are stamped eight hours behind
    universal time; Sunday's breakfast, at 8:09, is stamped seven. Daylight saving time had started at two that
    morning, and the phone had quietly moved itself forward an hour while we slept. Keep that in mind: it will
    matter in Arizona.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0313.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0313.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 2: from Pahrump to Badwater Basin, Devil's Golf Course, Artist's Palette and the Mesquite Flat Sand Dunes, then to the Las Vegas Strip." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: Badwater, Devil's Golf Course, Artist's Palette and the dunes, then the long drive to Las Vegas.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Badwater Basin</h3>
  <p>
    By 10:45 we were at <strong>Badwater Basin</strong>, 282 feet below sea level and the lowest place in North
    America. High on the cliff above the parking lot is a small sign marking where sea level would be, which is a
    neat way of making you feel how deep in the hole you are. The view from the lot was nothing like the brochure:
    instead of a dry white plain, a shallow pool of water stretched out from the boardwalk, edged with reeds and
    salt crust, reflecting a sky full of high, thin cloud. Across the valley, the Panamint Range had a dusting of snow
    on its peaks.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'badwater-pool.webp'} alt="A shallow pool with salt crust and reeds below the Badwater viewing area, with snow-dusted mountains across the valley." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Badwater's spring-fed pool, with snow on the Panamints.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'badwater-boardwalk.webp'} alt="The Badwater boardwalk leading out across the salt flat, with tiny figures walking on it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The boardwalk out onto the flats.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The water is why the place is called Badwater. The story goes that a surveyor's mule refused to drink from the
    pool, and the surveyor marked it on his map as "bad water". It isn't poisonous, just very salty: a small spring
    seeps up here, and the minerals it carries concentrate as it evaporates. Even so, the pool has its own residents.
    The Badwater snail, a tiny mollusk, lives in this spring and a few others nearby and nowhere else on Earth.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'badwater-mountain.webp'} alt="The steep brown face of the Black Mountains rising above the white salt crust at Badwater." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Black Mountains rise straight out of the salt.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Behind the parking lot, the Black Mountains go straight up from the salt in one steep brown wall. That
    abruptness is the whole story of Death Valley in one view. The valley floor is a block of crust that has been
    sinking along faults for millions of years while the mountains on either side rose, and the sediments washing
    into the gap have never been able to fill it. Water that drains in has no way out, so it evaporates, and leaves its
    salt behind. Do that for long enough and you get a salt flat of some two hundred square miles.
  </p>
  <p>
    A sign at the edge of the boardwalk explains the shapes in the crust: as the salt dries and recrystallizes, it
    pushes up into ridges that form polygons, like the cracks in drying mud but raised instead of sunken. Beyond the
    sign, the trail is simply a white strip of salt, packed smooth by thousands of feet, running straight out toward
    the middle of the valley. The farther you walk, the whiter, flatter and quieter it gets. The far mountains don't
    seem to get any closer, and the salt crunches faintly underfoot like old snow.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'badwater-sign.webp'} alt="An interpretive sign titled Salt Flats, set in rough salt crust." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A guide to the salt polygons.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'badwater-path.webp'} alt="A smooth white path of packed salt running straight across the salt flat toward distant mountains." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The salt path out toward the middle of the valley.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Devil's Golf Course</h3>
  <p>
    An hour later, a few miles up the valley, the same salt looks completely different. At <strong>Devil's Golf
    Course</strong> the crust has been broken up into a field of jagged, knee-high lumps of salt and mud, stretching
    to the mountains like a plowed field turned to stone. It got its name from a 1930s National Park Service guidebook,
    which observed that only the devil could play golf on such rough links. Wind and the occasional rain sculpt the salt
    into sharp spires and razor edges; it is the kind of ground you step on carefully, if at all. The park service says
    that on a quiet, hot day you can hear the crystals ping and crack as they expand in the heat.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'devils-golf-course.webp'} alt="A field of jagged salt pinnacles stretching to snow-dusted mountains under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Devil's Golf Course, 11:49 am. Bring your own clubs.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the way north we turned off at <strong>Artist's Palette</strong>, where the hillside is streaked with pink,
    green and turquoise.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'artists-palette.webp'} alt="Bare hills of tan and brown rock with patches of pale pink and turquoise, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Artist's Palette, 12:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The last stop in the valley was the <strong>Mesquite Flat Sand Dunes</strong> near Stovepipe Wells, at 1:36 in
    the afternoon: smooth, rippled dunes with mesquite bushes holding onto the sand at their edges, the classic
    desert of the movies in the middle of a park that is mostly rock and salt. The only photo from there has people in
    it, so you'll have to imagine the dunes.
  </p>

  <h3>Las Vegas at night</h3>
  <p>
    By a little after five we were coming up I-15 into Las Vegas, past the new football stadium and the gold towers
    of Mandalay Bay. Dinner was Chinese, with a whole roast duck, and then we went down the Strip to the MGM Grand to
    see <strong>David Copperfield</strong>. The next picture is at 11:11 that night, on the Strip, in front of
    <strong>New York-New York</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a fitting first Vegas photo, since nothing says "you are no longer in the desert" like a scaled-down
    Manhattan skyline lit up at midnight. The casino, which opened in 1997, is built as a cluster of towers modeled on
    the Empire State Building, the Chrysler Building and their neighbors, with a Statue of Liberty out front and a
    roller coaster looping around the whole thing. On the Strip at that hour there is no such thing as night: the
    palm trees are lit from below, the billboards are bright enough to read by, and the sidewalks are full.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nyny-night.webp'} alt="New York-New York's replica Manhattan skyline lit up at night, with palm trees and a roller coaster in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>New York-New York, 11:11 pm. Twelve hours earlier we were below sea level.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 3: Las Vegas to Page</h2>
  <p>
    Monday was a day of two halves: a morning on the Strip, then a long drive east into Arizona. It started at
    8:26 at the north end of the Strip, at <strong>Resorts World Las Vegas</strong>, where we had spent the night at the Las Vegas Hilton. The resort had
    opened the previous summer.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0314.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0314.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 3: Resorts World and the Bellagio in Las Vegas, then a drive east to Glen Canyon Dam and Page, Arizona." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: a morning on the Strip, then east to Page.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Resorts World was the first brand-new resort to open on the Strip in more than a decade, built on the site of the
    old Stardust, and it is impossible to miss: tall towers clad in glossy red panels and dark glass, with a giant LED
    pylon out front playing ads to the traffic. In the clear morning light, against a deep blue sky, the red looked
    almost unreal.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'resorts-world-red.webp'} alt="The red and dark-glass towers of Resorts World Las Vegas seen from below against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Resorts World, 8:27 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'resorts-world-pylon.webp'} alt="Resorts World's red tower with a tall LED pylon in front of it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tower and its giant screen.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Looking south from the sidewalk, one of the curved bronze towers of the Wynn stood alone down the boulevard,
    catching the sun. At half past eight on a Monday the Strip is a different street from the night before: half
    empty, the sprinklers running, traffic cones out, and the desert light making everything look like a scale
    model of itself.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wynn-encore-morning.webp'} alt="A curved bronze hotel tower at the end of a wide, nearly empty boulevard on a sunny morning." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Down the Strip toward the Wynn.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Lunch, around half past twelve, was the buffet at the <strong>Bellagio</strong>: a plate of sausages, mussels, noodles and roast carrots, then fruit, then a second plate entirely of small desserts,
    a blueberry tart, a mango square, panna cotta with raspberries, a pink doughnut and a chocolate one. A Vegas buffet
    is less a meal than a test of planning, and this one was handled with a clear strategy.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bellagio-plate.webp'} alt="A plate of sausages, mussels, noodles and carrots, with fruit and a glass of juice beside it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch at the Bellagio, round one.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bellagio-desserts.webp'} alt="A plate of small desserts: tart, mango square, panna cotta, and two doughnuts." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Round two.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Glen Canyon Dam at dusk</h3>
  <p>
    Then came the long drive east, our friend at the wheel. Las Vegas to Page is about 275 miles: up I-15 through
    the Virgin River Gorge to St. George, Utah, where we stopped for gas, then back down into Arizona. The camera
    roll records almost none of it; the photos simply stop at the Bellagio and start again at Glen Canyon. The one
    exception, at 6:56 pm, is a blurred shot through the car window of the canyon walls, taken as we crossed the
    bridge beside the dam.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Glen Canyon Dam</strong> is a 710-foot wall of concrete across the Colorado River, finished in the 1960s.
    Behind it is <strong>Lake Powell</strong>, the second-largest reservoir in the country, which flooded nearly 200
    miles of Glen Canyon. Page itself started life in 1957 as a camp for the people building the dam. We stopped on
    the rim just as the sun went down. The sky over the canyon turned yellow and orange along the horizon, the dam and
    the steel towers of its power lines went black against it, and the red rock below faded to brown and then to
    nothing.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'glen-canyon-dusk.webp'} alt="The silhouette of Glen Canyon Dam and power-line towers against an orange sunset, above dark red rock." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Glen Canyon Dam, and its power lines, at dusk.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was at Sunset 89 in Page at 9:45: burgers and fries, and one plate with a fried egg on top of the whole thing, the
    way a truck stop would do it if a truck stop were trying to impress you.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'page-dinner.webp'} alt="A plate of fries, shredded lettuce and a fried egg on a wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dinner in Page.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>An interlude on clocks</h3>
  <p>
    Now for the puzzle. The sunrise at Horseshoe Bend and the Antelope Canyon tour the next day both ran on a clock,
    and in this part of the world, which clock is a real question. Here is how it works.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most of the United States moved its clocks forward on March 13th. <strong>Arizona</strong> doesn't. It is too hot
    in summer for anyone to want an extra hour of evening sun, and the state has stayed on standard time all year round
    since 1968. In winter, Arizona shares a clock with Colorado and Utah; in summer, with California and Nevada. Our
    route the day before went up through Utah, so the official clock moved an hour forward at the Utah line and back
    again when we came down into Arizona, and the phone kept flipping after that: on that one drive its clock changed several times, and by the time we reached Page none of
    us was sure what time it was.
  </p>
  <p>
    But the <strong>Navajo Nation</strong>, the largest reservation in the country, covering the whole northeast
    corner of Arizona and spilling into Utah and New Mexico, does observe daylight saving time, so its clocks
    match New Mexico and Utah. From March to November it is an hour ahead of the rest of Arizona. And inside the
    Navajo Nation sits the Hopi Reservation, which doesn't observe it, making a hole in the middle of the hole. Page is
    not on Navajo land. Antelope Canyon, a few miles down the road, is. So the town and the canyon run an hour apart.
  </p>
  <p>
    The phone knew all this, or tried to. That evening at the dam, the photos are stamped seven hours behind
    universal time, Arizona's clock. An hour later, a few miles away near the Utah line, the phone has switched to
    six, probably picking up a cell tower across the Utah line. Half an hour after that it is back on seven. By 10:44 that night, a screenshot
    of a map of the region had been scribbled over with "+0" and "+1" around the Navajo Nation, which is how
    you know that somebody in the car had noticed. The next morning a world-clock screenshot shows the phone
    saying 6:26 while the line for Los Angeles says 5:26, though Page and Los Angeles were on the same time that day.
    In other words, the phone was running an hour ahead of the town it was sitting in.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 4: Horseshoe Bend, Lake Powell and Antelope Canyon</h2>
  <p>
    Tuesday, March 15th, after a night in Page, was the big day. In Page time, which was the right time for this part of the day, we were
    on the road by 6:29, in the blue light before dawn, and at the trailhead for <strong>Horseshoe Bend</strong> five
    minutes later.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0315.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0315.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 4: Horseshoe Bend at sunrise, the Wahweap Overlook, Lake Shore Drive and Wahweap Marina on Lake Powell, then Upper Antelope Canyon." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 4: a sunrise, a lake and a slot canyon, all before two in the afternoon.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Horseshoe Bend at sunrise</h3>
  <p>
    The trail starts with a big painted sign of the bend itself, standing alone in the red sand like a billboard
    for the view, in case anyone has driven out at dawn without knowing what they came for. From there a wide path of
    packed sand, edged with stones, runs about three-quarters of a mile over a low rise and down toward the rim. In
    the half-light the sand was a deep rusty orange, the brush was dark, and the sky was pale gold along the
    horizon fading to blue overhead. Ahead of us a line of other early risers was already walking toward the edge.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'horseshoe-sign.webp'} alt="A large painted sign of Horseshoe Bend standing in red desert sand at dawn." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The trailhead sign, 6:34 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'horseshoe-trail.webp'} alt="A sandy trail edged with stones leading across red desert toward a glowing horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The trail to the rim, just before sunrise.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And then, without any warning, the ground ends. You walk up to the railing and look down a thousand feet of
    sheer sandstone to the <strong>Colorado River</strong>, which comes toward you, swings all the way around a huge
    dome of rock in a near-perfect circle, and leaves in almost the direction it came from. At that hour the river
    was in deep shadow, a smooth dark green-blue with no visible current. It is very quiet up there. The river is
    too far down to hear.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'horseshoe-bend.webp'} alt="Horseshoe Bend at sunrise: the Colorado River curving around a sandstone dome far below, with the far rim glowing orange." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Horseshoe Bend, 6:42 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The overlook faces west, so at sunrise the sun comes up behind you. The first light didn't touch the river or
    the rock in the middle at all. It caught only the top of the far rim, a thin line of cliffs on the horizon that
    turned bright orange, almost fluorescent, while everything below it stayed cool and blue. Over the next few
    minutes the line of light crept down the far wall, and the pale sandstone of the dome went from gray to pink.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'horseshoe-rim.webp'} alt="Horseshoe Bend framed by boulders on the rim, with the river in shadow and orange light on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From a few steps along the rim, 6:43 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'horseshoe-morning.webp'} alt="Horseshoe Bend a few minutes later, with the sky brightening and the river still dark." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Five minutes later, the sky has caught up.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The bend is what geologists call an entrenched meander. Long ago, the river wandered in lazy loops across a flat
    plain, the way rivers do. Then the whole Colorado Plateau slowly rose, and the river cut down into the rock as the
    land came up, keeping its loops as it went, until each loop had become a canyon a thousand feet deep. The rock it
    cut is Navajo Sandstone, which formed from a field of sand dunes that covered this region roughly 190 million
    years ago; in the walls you can still see the slanting lines of the old dune faces. The water itself is
    unusually clear and cold, because it comes out of the bottom of Lake Powell, a few miles upstream, and leaves its
    silt behind in the reservoir.
  </p>

  <h3>Lake Powell from above</h3>
  <p>
    By 7:27 we were at the <strong>Wahweap Overlook</strong>, on a bluff just north of town, looking out over Lake
    Powell. It is a strange and beautiful view: deep blue water winding between benches and buttes of red and cream
    sandstone, a drowned landscape where the canyons have become bays and the mesas have become islands. With the
    sun still low in the east, the light came straight at us across the water, and the far shore was hazy and pale.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wahweap-overlook.webp'} alt="Lake Powell seen from the Wahweap Overlook: blue bays among sandstone benches, with desert scrub in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lake Powell from the Wahweap Overlook.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wahweap-bay.webp'} alt="A deep blue bay of Lake Powell enclosed by red and white sandstone cliffs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A bay below Lake Shore Drive, 7:41 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From there we drove slowly along <strong>Lake Shore Drive</strong>, stopping at pullouts above the water. Look
    closely at those photos and you'll see the most striking thing about the lake that spring: the pale band around
    every shoreline, like the ring left in a bathtub. That bleached rock is where the water used to be. After two
    decades of drought on the Colorado River, the lake was far below full, and around the very days we were there it
    dropped below 3,525 feet above sea level for the first time since it was filled. That level had been set as a
    warning line to protect the dam's ability to generate power. The view was gorgeous, and it was also a gauge.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lake-shore-drive.webp'} alt="A wide view of Lake Powell's blue water with white bathtub-ring bands on the sandstone shores and red desert in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bathtub ring along Lake Shore Drive, 7:49 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The last stop by the lake was above <strong>Wahweap Marina</strong>, where hundreds of houseboats sat in rows on
    their docks, white rectangles on blue water, waiting for summer. Renting a houseboat and disappearing up the side
    canyons of Lake Powell for a week is a classic Southwestern vacation. In March, at eight in the morning, the
    marina was perfectly still.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wahweap-marina.webp'} alt="Rows of houseboats at Wahweap Marina on Lake Powell, seen from a hill with the morning sun above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Houseboats at Wahweap Marina, 8:10 am.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Into Upper Antelope Canyon</h3>
  <p>
    Then, at midday, the main event. <strong>Upper Antelope Canyon</strong> lies on Navajo land just east of Page,
    and you can only visit with a Navajo guide. The tour starts with a ride in an open-air truck: rows of padded
    benches under a canopy on the back of a pickup, which carried us out of town and then turned off the highway into
    the dry sandy bed of Antelope Creek. The wash is wide and flat, its sand a deep orange, and tire tracks run along
    it in every direction. The truck rolls and slides through it, the canopy rattling, the air dry and full of
    fine sand, between low banks of wind-carved sandstone. The first photo from the ride is stamped 12:08. That is
    Navajo time; by the clocks in Page it was 11:08.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-truck.webp'} alt="The empty benches of an open-air tour truck under a canopy, looking out over a sandy desert wash." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tour truck, heading up the wash.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-wash.webp'} alt="A wide sandy wash with tire tracks, between red sandstone slopes under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The dry bed of Antelope Creek.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The truck stops where the wash runs up against a wall of rock. From the outside, the canyon is nothing: a great
    rounded bluff of red sandstone, carved into bulges and hollows by the wind, with a narrow dark crack at its base.
    You'd walk right past it. That crack is the entrance. Groups gather at the mouth and wait their turn, and then a
    guide leads each one in.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-outside.webp'} alt="Rounded red sandstone bluffs at the end of the sandy wash, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The outside of the canyon: just a bluff.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-entrance.webp'} alt="The narrow entrance to Upper Antelope Canyon, with smooth pink and red sandstone walls curving inward." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The entrance, 12:38 pm Navajo time.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Step through, and the world changes. The canyon is a slot: a crack in the sandstone, perhaps a hundred feet deep
    and in places only an arm's length wide, which you walk along on a floor of soft sand. Its walls are not straight
    but flowing, swelling and curving and twisting around each other, carved into smooth hollows and sharp fins. The
    Navajo name for it means "the place where water runs through rocks", and that is exactly how it was made. For a few
    hours a year, flash floods from storms miles away come roaring down the wash, squeeze into this crack, and scour it
    out with sand. Between floods, the wind finishes the polishing. The result looks less like rock than like something
    poured, frozen in the middle of moving.
  </p>
  <p>
    Look straight up and you see the sky as a crooked blue river at the top of the walls. That photo, oddly, is
    stamped in Arizona time while the ones around it are in Navajo time. Even deep in a slot canyon, the phone was
    still arguing with itself about what time it was.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-sky.webp'} alt="Looking straight up between curving red canyon walls to a narrow strip of blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sky from the canyon floor.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Light and color</h3>
  <p>
    What makes Antelope Canyon famous is the light. Almost no direct sunlight reaches the floor. Instead it falls
    into the top of the slot and bounces from wall to wall on its way down, and each bounce picks up the color of the
    sandstone. So the light changes as you walk: near the openings it is a hot, glowing gold, a little deeper it turns
    orange and salmon, and in the darkest corners it becomes violet and even blue. None of these colors are filters.
    The rock is all roughly the same red; the color is in the light.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-glow.webp'} alt="Canyon walls glowing gold and orange where light enters from above, fading to violet in the shadows." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Gold near the openings.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-purple.webp'} alt="A canyon wall of sweeping layered sandstone lit in deep pink and violet." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Violet where the light has bounced the most.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The walls themselves are covered in fine parallel lines, the layering of the ancient sand dunes that became the
    Navajo Sandstone. Because the floods carved across those layers at every angle, the lines sweep and curve with the
    walls, so the whole canyon looks like it has been brushed in one direction. Some of the overhangs look like waves
    about to break; others look like folds of fabric, or flames, or wood grain. Everyone sees something different. The
    guides have names for a few of the shapes, and they point them out as you go.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-flame.webp'} alt="Dark canyon walls framing a bright orange opening where sunlight pours in from above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Where sunlight meets the slot.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It is not a lonely place. On a typical day the tours run back to back, groups move along the sand in single
    file, and the Navajo guides are famous for knowing exactly which phone settings bring out the colors. But between
    groups there is a stillness to it, the cool, sheltered stillness of being underground, with the bright desert just
    a hundred feet above your head.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-blue.webp'} alt="Looking up through a twisting slot in the rock, with the walls lit blue and a bright white opening in the middle." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking up, where the walls turn blue.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-arch.webp'} alt="Sweeping canyon walls forming a dark arch around a golden patch of light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An arch of sandstone around a patch of sun.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Deeper in, the canyon opens into small chambers and closes again, and the light comes in through skylights in the
    rock: here a narrow slit, there a ragged hole with a golden halo around it. The walls in these places curl up and
    over your head into shapes that would be hard to model on a computer, which is saying something.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-amber.webp'} alt="Amber-lit canyon walls rising to a bright opening, with layered sandstone in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Amber light in one of the chambers.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-ridge.webp'} alt="A thin ridge of pale sandstone arching over a dark gap, lit from behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A fin of rock against the light.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In one stretch, the walls are banded so strongly that they look like a stack of stone ribbons, white and lavender
    against black. This is the same layering as everywhere else, just cut at a different angle and lit from the
    side.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-waves.webp'} alt="Sweeping horizontal bands of pale sandstone like frozen waves against dark shadow." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Frozen waves, 1:08 pm Navajo time.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The beams</h3>
  <p>
    Then came the famous part. Around midday in spring and summer, the sun is high enough to shine straight down
    through a few gaps in the roof of the slot, all the way to the floor. In the dusty air of the canyon each of these
    shafts of sun shows up as a solid-looking beam of white light, standing on the sand like a column. The beams only
    appear for a couple of hours a day and only for part of the year. Mid-March is the very start of the season,
    and this is why the tours are timed by the clock so precisely, and why getting the clock wrong would matter.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-beam.webp'} alt="A narrow shaft of pale blue-white sunlight falling between pink sandstone walls to the canyon floor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A light beam, 1:11 pm Navajo time.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    To make the beams really show, the guides throw sand into them. A handful of sand tossed up the wall falls through
    the shaft of light and hangs there for a few seconds as a glowing, sparkling cloud, then drifts down and piles up
    on the floor in a little white heap. It is a trick that has been done thousands of times a day for years, and it is
    still mesmerizing: for a moment the light itself looks like something you could pick up.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-sand-pour.webp'} alt="Sand thrown into a beam of sunlight falling into an orange slot canyon, glowing white as it hits the floor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sand in the beam, 1:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-sand-beam.webp'} alt="A beam of light filled with drifting sand angling down between glowing orange canyon walls." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A few seconds later, the dust still hanging in the light.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Toward the far end of the canyon the light changes again. The walls narrow, the slot gets taller, and the colors
    go from orange to a deep crimson, with bright pink edges where the sun catches the rim. In one last bend, the
    light from a single high opening turned the walls an icy blue, the deepest color of the day.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-crimson.webp'} alt="A tall crimson slot with a bright pink opening at the top." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Crimson, near the far end, 1:19 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-blue-slot.webp'} alt="A tall narrow slot lit in icy blue and white against near-black walls." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>And blue, a minute later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And then, at 1:24 Navajo time, we were out. Rather than walking back through the canyon, the route climbs out of
    the far end: a steep steel staircase, painted red, goes straight up from the slickrock, and a long covered
    catwalk leads back across the top of the bluff. After an hour in the shade, the sunlight on the open rock was
    blinding, the sky was an unbroken deep blue, and the sandstone around the stairs was the same wind-carved swirl
    as the walls below, only in plain daylight. It looked almost ordinary. It's hard to believe the canyon is down
    there.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-slickrock.webp'} alt="Wind-carved red sandstone slickrock under a deep blue sky, outside the canyon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Back in the sun, on top of the canyon.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The stairs are a feat of their own: a narrow steel flight, set almost vertically against the rock, with a
    sheet-metal roof over each landing. From the bottom they seem to go straight into the sky. The catwalk, a long
    metal walkway along the rim, then carried us back over the sand toward the trucks.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-stairs.webp'} alt="A steep red steel staircase with a roof climbing up out of the canyon into a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The way out.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope-catwalk.webp'} alt="A covered steel catwalk with red railings running across the sandstone above the canyon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The catwalk back across the top, 1:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    So, after all that, did the clocks cause any trouble? The photos say the tour truck left at 11:08 Arizona time,
    and the canyon visit ran from about 11:35 to 12:25, which is right in the middle of the window for the light
    beams. If there was any confusion on the morning, it doesn't show in the photos. By 2:38 pm, back in Page, the
    phone had settled on Arizona time again, and stayed there.
  </p>

  <h2>Back to Las Vegas</h2>
  <p>
    That afternoon we drove back to Las Vegas, the same 275 miles in reverse, and by 7:18 pm we were on the freeway
    into the city. At 7:37 the first thing in the windshield was <strong>The Strat</strong>, its tower lit up gold
    against the night, at the north end of the Strip.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-vegas.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-vegas.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Days 4 and 5 in Las Vegas: The Strat at night, Chinatown, the airport, brunch at the Wynn, Resorts World and the High Roller." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 4–5: Las Vegas from above, at night and by day.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>The Strat, at night</h3>
  <p>
    The Strat (originally the Stratosphere) is an 1,149-foot concrete tower with a pod of observation decks,
    restaurants and thrill rides on top. Opened in 1996, it is the tallest freestanding observation tower in the
    United States. From the road at night it is pure showbiz: a gold-lit shaft rising from a white hotel complex, the
    pod at the top glowing like a crown, with the neon of the old north Strip all around its base.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'strat-night.webp'} alt="The Strat tower lit up gold at night, rising above a lit white hotel building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Strat from the car, 7:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A little after nine we were at the top. From the observation deck, the city lies spread out underneath you in
    every direction, a vast flat grid of orange streetlights that runs all the way to the dark mountains on the
    horizon, perfectly straight roads ruled across it. The Strip is the brightest line on the grid, heading south from
    the foot of the tower toward the airport. At this height the city is completely silent. Up close it is
    all noise and flashing signs; from more than 800 feet up it is just a carpet of light, slowly twinkling.
  </p>
  <p>
    At the edge of the deck, a giant yellow steel arm sticks out over the drop: one of the thrill rides that hang
    people off the side of the tower. We stuck to the railings.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'strat-view.webp'} alt="Las Vegas at night from the top of The Strat: a grid of lights stretching to the horizon beyond a railing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Las Vegas from the top of The Strat, 9:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'strat-ride.webp'} alt="The yellow steel arm of a thrill ride extending over the lights of Las Vegas at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A thrill ride's arm, over the Strip.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The day ended, at 10:21 pm, a few miles west in Las Vegas's Chinatown, at Hachi, a Japanese izakaya: skewers with a
    wedge of lemon, then a tray of chicken. Considering that the day had started at Horseshoe Bend before sunrise, this
    was a reasonable reward.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'chinatown-skewers.webp'} alt="A tray of grilled chicken skewers on a dark wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Skewers in Chinatown, 10:31 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 5: the last day in Vegas</h2>
  <p>
    On the last morning, Wednesday, March 16th, we returned the car at the airport's rental car center at a quarter
    to nine. By mid-morning we were back at The Strat, where we had spent the night. By day, from its
    base, the tower is an entirely different object: a plain white concrete shaft that goes straight up into a deep
    blue sky, the pod at the top tiny and dark. You have to tilt your head all the way back to see it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'strat-day.webp'} alt="The white concrete Strat tower seen from directly below against a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Strat by day, 11:01 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Brunch, at half past twelve, was at the <strong>Wynn</strong>, which Steve Wynn opened in 2005 on the site of
    the old Desert Inn. The restaurant was bright and busy, and the food was brunch done
    properly: strawberry smoothies with a whole strawberry on a stick, eggs Benedict, crisp potatoes, and a stack of
    pancakes buried in berries, mint and cream.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wynn-smoothie.webp'} alt="A tall strawberry smoothie topped with a whole strawberry on a stick." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Brunch at the Wynn.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wynn-pancakes.webp'} alt="Pancakes topped with berries, mint and cream on a white plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pancakes, with most of a fruit stand on top.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Resorts World, again</h3>
  <p>
    Mid-afternoon took us back to <strong>Resorts World</strong>, which had looked so dramatic two mornings
    earlier. In the afternoon light, with big white clouds rolling over from the west, the huge LED wall on the side
    of the tower was showing an animation of glowing orange shapes. The red sign at the entrance, in front of a
    garden of desert trees, is clearly designed to be photographed, and we obliged.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'resorts-world-sign.webp'} alt="The red Resorts World Las Vegas sign in a garden in front of the resort's red-edged towers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Resorts World sign, 2:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'resorts-world-screen.webp'} alt="Resorts World's towers with a giant LED wall showing an orange animation, under a sky of white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The tower's LED wall.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Across the street, the north end of the Strip showed what Las Vegas looks like between projects: tall towers in
    bronze and dark glass, separated by empty lots and long stretches of fence, with big desert clouds sailing over
    all of it. The Strip is always building something and always tearing something down, and this end of it has more
    gaps than most.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'strip-towers.webp'} alt="Tall dark and bronze towers at the north end of the Las Vegas Strip, under a blue sky with white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The north Strip's towers, from Resorts World.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Inside, Resorts World is a newer kind of casino. The ceilings are high and coffered, lit with soft gold, and the
    carpet is a bold black-and-white zigzag instead of the traditional dizzying swirl. The centerpiece of the
    atrium is a giant glass sphere covered in a projected artwork, a glowing ball of purple, red and blue several
    times taller than a person. The slot machines, as everywhere else in town, are the same as always.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'resorts-world-sphere.webp'} alt="A huge glass sphere covered with a colorful purple and red projected artwork inside Resorts World." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sphere in the atrium.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'resorts-world-casino.webp'} alt="A casino floor with slot machines under a coffered gold-lit ceiling and a zigzag carpet." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The casino floor, 3:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The High Roller</h3>
  <p>
    The last stop of the trip was the <strong>High Roller</strong>, the giant observation wheel behind the LINQ in
    the middle of the Strip. It is 550 feet tall and, when it opened in 2014, it was the tallest observation wheel in
    the world, a title it kept until Dubai built a bigger one in 2021. It turns so slowly that it never stops: you step
    into a glass cabin the size of a small room while it is moving, and one revolution takes about half an hour. We
    boarded at 4:19 pm, and the photos run until 4:40.
  </p>
  <p>
    As the cabin rises, the wheel itself becomes part of the view. Looking up through the glass you see the huge
    white rim and its cables, like the spokes of an enormous bicycle wheel, with the other cabins hanging off it.
    Looking down, you see the parking lots and the LINQ promenade dropping away.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'high-roller-spokes.webp'} alt="The white rim and cable spokes of the High Roller seen from inside a glass cabin, with Las Vegas far below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The wheel's spokes, from inside the cabin.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'high-roller-cabin.webp'} alt="Another High Roller cabin passing beside the Harrah's and Venetian hotels, with storm clouds over the mountains." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A neighboring cabin, passing Harrah's.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Near the top, the whole middle of the Strip lies below you. Directly underneath are Harrah's and, beyond it, the
    Venetian with its campanile; across the road is the pink-and-white bulk of the Flamingo, the oldest resort still
    operating on the Strip, with a garden and pools tucked into the middle of its towers. Farther south, the
    Bellagio's long curved front and the glass towers beyond it line up in the haze, and beyond them all, the
    mountains. That afternoon a squall was building over the mountains, with rain hanging below
    the cloud in long gray streaks, so the light changed every minute.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'high-roller-harrahs.webp'} alt="Harrah's and the Venetian from the High Roller, with storm clouds and rain over the mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Harrah's and the Venetian, with a squall over the mountains.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It is a good way to end a trip like this one. Four days earlier we had been looking down from Dante's View into the
    lowest valley on the continent, and the day before that from the rim of Horseshoe Bend. Here was one more view
    from above, of a city that only exists because of water brought across the desert, much of it from the same
    river that carved the bend. Las Vegas gets most of its water from Lake Mead, the reservoir behind Hoover Dam, a
    few hundred miles downstream of Lake Powell's bathtub ring.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'high-roller-flamingo.webp'} alt="The Flamingo hotel and the towers of the central Strip from the High Roller, with mountains and clouds beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Flamingo and the central Strip from the top of the wheel, 4:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The cabin came back down to the platform at 4:40, and that is the last photo of the trip. That evening we flew home
    to Los Angeles.
  </p>

  <h2>Five days, three clocks</h2>
  <p>
    Looking back at it now, what stands out is how much the landscape changed for such a short trip. In five days we
    went from salt flats below sea level to a thousand-foot cliff, from the dry clay hills of an ancient lake to a
    reservoir that was running dry, and from a canyon too narrow to stretch your arms in to a city laid out flat to the
    horizon. Death Valley is a landscape made by the ground sinking; Glen Canyon and Horseshoe Bend, by the ground
    rising and a river cutting down to keep up; Antelope Canyon, by sudden floods that last a few hours a year.
    And Las Vegas, by people who decided that none of that would stop them.
  </p>
  <p>
    And then there are the clocks. Most people never notice their phone changing time zones. On this trip the
    photos' clock jumped at least seven times in three days, and only one of those jumps was daylight saving. A few were real state lines; the rest were the
    phone flipping back and forth between Arizona time, Navajo time and Utah time, depending on which cell tower it
    happened to hear. The
    photos remember all of it, faithfully, to the second. It is a small reminder that "what time is it?" is not
    quite the simple question it seems, and that in a few corners of the country, the honest answer is "it depends
    which side of the road you're on".
  </p>

